### 2. The fix, and the honest boundary it draws

The gate is right that shape can never establish authenticity — genuine references are plain maps (`engine.cljc:316 issue-ref`), and authenticity is the heap+seal check. I verified empirically (spliced-`cell/new` probes on both JVM evaluators) that the only in-language probe is `cell/get` itself, whose `check-ref!` fails by **host ex-info that no guest construct can catch** (the `try` lowering catches outcome-tagged guest throws only; `u/capture` escapes carry guest conditions only; every backend steps the effect inline with no catch). So "fall back when the authenticity check fails" is **inexpressible in-language** — that needs a yin/vm non-fatal probe (e.g. a `cell/live?` primitive), which this round's boundaries forbid. Dead ends examined and closed: shape/format tests (the gate's rejected class), registries of seen refs (callback args unreachable), deref-first-then-data (genuine refs invoked with keys they contain answer wrongly), custom effect handlers from the prelude (not portable to remote harnesses), FFI (no heap access).

What landed instead is the strongest *sound* discriminator: the engine mints **every** heap id from one counter as `:cell-N` (`gen-id`), so a full-shape map whose `:id` is not a keyword of that language **provably names no cell** — it can never be genuine, and it routes to ordinary map invocation (get on the key) on both evaluators. Every genuine reference passes the test (its `:id` *is* `:cell-N`), so admission still receives exactly the genuine candidates: live entries dereference through to the call; genuine-but-dead keeps the standing `not-callable` refusal byte-for-byte. **Residual, pinned as a test:** a forged `:id :cell-7` + 64-char-seal map still fails closed at `check-ref!` (`:dead-or-forged-reference`) — the same fate any forged reference meets anywhere in the system; nothing gains capability. The commit message carries this analysis verbatim.

### 3. Conflict resolution narrative (rebase)

All conflicts were in `src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc`; both lanes rewrote the same three regions:
